{"track":"act2l2","num":"1.01","slug":"complex-numbers","title":"Complex Numbers","subtitle":"ClipSAT Printable Practice Suite (clipsat.org) • Topic 1.01 • 20 FRQs + 25 MCQs • Difficulty Progression • Cross-Lesson Synthesis","sections":[{"heading":"Part I — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","questions":[{"q":"Find the modulus and principal argument of $z = -2\\sqrt{3} + 2i$, and express $z$ in polar form $r(\\cos\\theta + i\\sin\\theta)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","answer":"Step 1: Compute modulus $r$: $r = \\sqrt{(-2\\sqrt{3})^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{12 + 4} = \\sqrt{16} = 4.$ Step 2: Determine argument $\\theta$: Since $a = -2\\sqrt{3} < 0$ and $b = 2 > 0$, $z$ lies in Quadrant II. Reference angle: $\\tan\\alpha = \\left|\\frac{2}{-2\\sqrt{3}}\\right| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\Rightarrow \\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$ (or $30^\\circ$). Quadrant II angle: $\\theta = \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ (or $150^\\circ$). Step 3: Write polar form: $z = \\mathbf{4\\left(\\cos\\frac{5\\pi}{6} + i\\sin\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)}.$","explanation":"Step 1: Compute modulus $r$: $r = \\sqrt{(-2\\sqrt{3})^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{12 + 4} = \\sqrt{16} = 4.$ Step 2: Determine argument $\\theta$: Since $a = -2\\sqrt{3} < 0$ and $b = 2 > 0$, $z$ lies in Quadrant II. Reference angle: $\\tan\\alpha = \\left|\\frac{2}{-2\\sqrt{3}}\\right| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\Rightarrow \\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$ (or $30^\\circ$). Quadrant II angle: $\\theta = \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ (or $150^\\circ$). Step 3: Write polar form: $z = \\mathbf{4\\left(\\cos\\frac{5\\pi}{6} + i\\sin\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)}.$","figure":{"type":"circle","center":[0,0],"radius":6,"center_label":"$O$","radius_label":"$r = 6$","sector":{"start_deg":0,"end_deg":60,"label":"$60^\\circ$"},"chord":null,"width":210}},{"q":"Evaluate each power of $i$, using $i^2=-1$ and the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every $4$:","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Powers of $i$","parts":["$i^3$","$i^4$","$i^7$","$i^{22}$"],"cols":4,"answers":["$= -i$","$= 1$","$= -i$","$= -1$"],"answer":"(a) $= -i$; (b) $= 1$; (c) $= -i$; (d) $= -1$","explanation":"(a) $= -i$; (b) $= 1$; (c) $= -i$; (d) $= -1$","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["0.5*(x - (0))**2 + (-1)","0.5*x + 1"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":-1,"label":"$(0,-1)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $i^{101}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Powers of $i$","answer":"$101 = 4(25)+1$, so $i^{101}=(i^4)^{25}\\cdot i^1 = 1\\cdot i = i$.","explanation":"$101 = 4(25)+1$, so $i^{101}=(i^4)^{25}\\cdot i^1 = 1\\cdot i = i$."},{"q":"Let $z_1=3+4i$ and $z_2=-2+5i$. Find $z_1+z_2$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Addition and subtraction","answer":"$= 1+9i$","explanation":"$= 1+9i$"},{"q":"Let $z_1=2-3i$ and $z_2=-1-4i$. Find $z_1+z_2$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Addition and subtraction","answer":"$= 1-7i$","explanation":"$= 1-7i$"},{"q":"Find $(7+2i)-(3-5i)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Addition and subtraction","answer":"$= 4+7i$","explanation":"$= 4+7i$","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["0.5*(x - (1))**2 + (-1)","0.5*x + 1"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"point_labels":[{"x":1,"y":-1,"label":"$(1,-1)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Find $(4-i)-(-2+6i)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Addition and subtraction","answer":"$= 6-7i$","explanation":"$= 6-7i$"}]},{"heading":"Part II — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","questions":[{"q":"Multiply $(2+3i)(1-4i)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiplication","answer":"$(2)(1)-(3)(-4) + \\left[(2)(-4)+(3)(1)\\right]i = 14 - 5i$","explanation":"$(2)(1)-(3)(-4) + \\left[(2)(-4)+(3)(1)\\right]i = 14 - 5i$"},{"q":"Multiply $(-1+2i)(3+i)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiplication","answer":"$(-1)(3)-(2)(1) + \\left[(-1)(1)+(2)(3)\\right]i = -5+5i$","explanation":"$(-1)(3)-(2)(1) + \\left[(-1)(1)+(2)(3)\\right]i = -5+5i$"},{"q":"Multiply $(5-2i)(5+2i)$, and explain why the result has no imaginary part.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiplication","answer":"$= 25-(-4) + 0i = 29$. It is a product of conjugates $z\\bar z=|z|^2$ (Theorem 1.1), which is always real.","explanation":"$= 25-(-4) + 0i = 29$. It is a product of conjugates $z\\bar z=|z|^2$ (Theorem 1.1), which is always real.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["0.5*(x - (-1))**2 + (-1)","0.5*x + 1"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"point_labels":[{"x":-1,"y":-1,"label":"$(-1,-1)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"For $z=6-8i$, find $\\bar z$ and $|z|$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Conjugate and modulus","answer":"$\\bar z = 6+8i$, and $|z|=\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}=\\sqrt{100}=10$.","explanation":"$\\bar z = 6+8i$, and $|z|=\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}=\\sqrt{100}=10$."},{"q":"For $z=-5+12i$, find $\\bar z$ and $|z|$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Conjugate and modulus","answer":"$\\bar z = -5-12i$, and $|z|=\\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=\\sqrt{169}=13$.","explanation":"$\\bar z = -5-12i$, and $|z|=\\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=\\sqrt{169}=13$."},{"q":"Show that $z+\\bar z$ is always real for $z=a+bi$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Conjugate and modulus","answer":"$z+\\bar z = (a+bi)+(a-bi) = 2a$, which is real since $a$ is real.","explanation":"$z+\\bar z = (a+bi)+(a-bi) = 2a$, which is real since $a$ is real."},{"q":"Simplify $\\frac{3+4i}{1-2i}$ by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Division","answer":"Multiply by $\\frac{1+2i}{1+2i}$: numerator $= (3)(1)-(4)(2)+[(3)(2)+(4)(1)]i = -5+10i$; denominator $=1^2+2^2=5$. So the quotient is $-1+2i$.","explanation":"Multiply by $\\frac{1+2i}{1+2i}$: numerator $= (3)(1)-(4)(2)+[(3)(2)+(4)(1)]i = -5+10i$; denominator $=1^2+2^2=5$. So the quotient is $-1+2i$."}]},{"heading":"Part III — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","questions":[{"q":"Simplify $\\frac{1+i}{1-i}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Division","cross_lesson":"6.01 Limits","answer":"Multiply by $\\frac{1+i}{1+i}$: numerator $=(1)(1)-(1)(1)+[(1)(1)+(1)(1)]i = 0+2i$; denominator $=1^2+1^2=2$. So the quotient is $i$.","explanation":"Multiply by $\\frac{1+i}{1+i}$: numerator $=(1)(1)-(1)(1)+[(1)(1)+(1)(1)]i = 0+2i$; denominator $=1^2+1^2=2$. So the quotient is $i$."},{"q":"Simplify $\\frac{7-i}{3+2i}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Division","cross_lesson":"2.01 Matrices & Vectors","answer":"Multiply by $\\frac{3-2i}{3-2i}$: numerator $=(7)(3)-(-1)(-2)+[(7)(-2)+(-1)(3)]i = 19-17i$; denominator $=3^2+2^2=13$. So the quotient is $\\frac{19}{13}-\\frac{17}{13}i$.","explanation":"Multiply by $\\frac{3-2i}{3-2i}$: numerator $=(7)(3)-(-1)(-2)+[(7)(-2)+(-1)(3)]i = 19-17i$; denominator $=3^2+2^2=13$. So the quotient is $\\frac{19}{13}-\\frac{17}{13}i$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["0.5*(x - (-1))**2 + (-1)","0.5*x + 1"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"point_labels":[{"x":-1,"y":-1,"label":"$(-1,-1)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Solve $x^2+9=0$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Quadratic equations with complex roots","cross_lesson":"3.01 Sequences & Series","answer":"$x^2=-9 \\Rightarrow x=\\pm3i$.","explanation":"$x^2=-9 \\Rightarrow x=\\pm3i$."},{"q":"Solve $x^2-6x+13=0$ using the quadratic formula.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Quadratic equations with complex roots","cross_lesson":"4.01 Advanced Functions & Equations","answer":"Discriminant $=36-52=-16$, so $\\sqrt{-16}=4i$. $x=\\frac{6\\pm4i}{2}=3\\pm2i$.","explanation":"Discriminant $=36-52=-16$, so $\\sqrt{-16}=4i$. $x=\\frac{6\\pm4i}{2}=3\\pm2i$."},{"q":"Solve $2x^2+2x+5=0$ using the quadratic formula.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Quadratic equations with complex roots","cross_lesson":"5.01 Trigonometry & Polar Coordinates","answer":"Discriminant $=4-40=-36$, so $\\sqrt{-36}=6i$. $x=\\frac{-2\\pm6i}{4}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm\\frac{3}{2}i$.","explanation":"Discriminant $=4-40=-36$, so $\\sqrt{-36}=6i$. $x=\\frac{-2\\pm6i}{4}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm\\frac{3}{2}i$."},{"q":"Simplify $\\frac{3+4i}{1-2i}$ by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","cross_lesson":"6.01 Limits","answer":"Multiply by $\\frac{1+2i}{1+2i}$: numerator $= (3)(1)-(4)(2)+[(3)(2)+(4)(1)]i = -5+10i$; denominator $=1^2+2^2=5$. So the quotient is $-1+2i$.","explanation":"Multiply by $\\frac{1+2i}{1+2i}$: numerator $= (3)(1)-(4)(2)+[(3)(2)+(4)(1)]i = -5+10i$; denominator $=1^2+2^2=5$. So the quotient is $-1+2i$."}]},{"heading":"Part IV — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","questions":[{"q":"Evaluate $i^{130}$, using the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every 4.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Powers of $i$","choices":["$i$","$-i$","$1$","$-1$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$130=4(32)+2$, so $i^{130}=(i^4)^{32}\\cdot i^{2}=1\\cdot -1=-1$.","explanation":"$130=4(32)+2$, so $i^{130}=(i^4)^{32}\\cdot i^{2}=1\\cdot -1=-1$."},{"q":"Evaluate $i^{211}$, using the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every 4.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Addition and subtraction","choices":["$1$","$-1$","$-i$","$i$"],"correct":2,"answer":"$211=4(52)+3$, so $i^{211}=(i^4)^{52}\\cdot i^{3}=1\\cdot -i=-i$.","explanation":"$211=4(52)+3$, so $i^{211}=(i^4)^{52}\\cdot i^{3}=1\\cdot -i=-i$."},{"q":"Evaluate $i^{229}$, using the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every 4.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiplication","choices":["$1$","$i$","$-1$","$-i$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$229=4(57)+1$, so $i^{229}=(i^4)^{57}\\cdot i^{1}=1\\cdot i=i$.","explanation":"$229=4(57)+1$, so $i^{229}=(i^4)^{57}\\cdot i^{1}=1\\cdot i=i$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["0.5*(x - (0))**2 + (-2)","0.5*x + 1"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":-2,"label":"$(0,-2)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $i^{206}$, using the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every 4.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Conjugate and modulus","choices":["$i$","$-i$","$-1$","$1$"],"correct":2,"answer":"$206=4(51)+2$, so $i^{206}=(i^4)^{51}\\cdot i^{2}=1\\cdot -1=-1$.","explanation":"$206=4(51)+2$, so $i^{206}=(i^4)^{51}\\cdot i^{2}=1\\cdot -1=-1$."},{"q":"Evaluate $i^{62}$, using the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every 4.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Division","choices":["$i$","$-1$","$1$","$-i$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$62=4(15)+2$, so $i^{62}=(i^4)^{15}\\cdot i^{2}=1\\cdot -1=-1$.","explanation":"$62=4(15)+2$, so $i^{62}=(i^4)^{15}\\cdot i^{2}=1\\cdot -1=-1$."},{"q":"Evaluate $i^{101}$, using the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every 4.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Quadratic equations with complex roots","choices":["$-i$","$-1$","$i$","$1$"],"correct":2,"answer":"$101=4(25)+1$, so $i^{101}=(i^4)^{25}\\cdot i^{1}=1\\cdot i=i$.","explanation":"$101=4(25)+1$, so $i^{101}=(i^4)^{25}\\cdot i^{1}=1\\cdot i=i$."},{"q":"Evaluate $i^{31}$, using the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every 4.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","choices":["$-1$","$i$","$1$","$-i$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$31=4(7)+3$, so $i^{31}=(i^4)^{7}\\cdot i^{3}=1\\cdot -i=-i$.","explanation":"$31=4(7)+3$, so $i^{31}=(i^4)^{7}\\cdot i^{3}=1\\cdot -i=-i$."},{"q":"Evaluate $i^{178}$, using the fact that powers of $i$ repeat every 4.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","choices":["$i$","$1$","$-1$","$-i$"],"correct":2,"answer":"$178=4(44)+2$, so $i^{178}=(i^4)^{44}\\cdot i^{2}=1\\cdot -1=-1$.","explanation":"$178=4(44)+2$, so $i^{178}=(i^4)^{44}\\cdot i^{2}=1\\cdot -1=-1$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["0.5*(x - (-1))**2 + (-1)","0.5*x + 1"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"point_labels":[{"x":-1,"y":-1,"label":"$(-1,-1)$"}],"width":230}}]},{"heading":"Part V — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","questions":[{"q":"Multiply $(3-i)(-3-9i)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","choices":["$-18-24i$","$-18-30i$","$-24i$","$-18$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$(3)(-3)-(-1)(-9)+\\left[(3)(-9)+(-1)(-3)\\right]i=-18-24i$.","explanation":"$(3)(-3)-(-1)(-9)+\\left[(3)(-9)+(-1)(-3)\\right]i=-18-24i$."},{"q":"Multiply $(7+2i)(4)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$29+8i$","$28+8i$","$28-8i$","$28+28i$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$(7)(4)-(2)(0)+\\left[(7)(0)+(2)(4)\\right]i=28+8i$.","explanation":"$(7)(4)-(2)(0)+\\left[(7)(0)+(2)(4)\\right]i=28+8i$."},{"q":"Multiply $(7-6i)(4+8i)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["$-20+32i$","$76+80i$","$76-20i$","$76+32i$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$(7)(4)-(-6)(8)+\\left[(7)(8)+(-6)(4)\\right]i=76+32i$.","explanation":"$(7)(4)-(-6)(8)+\\left[(7)(8)+(-6)(4)\\right]i=76+32i$."},{"q":"Multiply $(-3-7i)(1-3i)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","choices":["$-24+18i$","$-24+2i$","$18+2i$","$-24+16i$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$(-3)(1)-(-7)(-3)+\\left[(-3)(-3)+(-7)(1)\\right]i=-24+2i$.","explanation":"$(-3)(1)-(-7)(-3)+\\left[(-3)(-3)+(-7)(1)\\right]i=-24+2i$."},{"q":"Multiply $(8-5i)(4+6i)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Powers of $i$","choices":["$62+2i$","$62+68i$","$62+28i$","$2+28i$"],"correct":2,"answer":"$(8)(4)-(-5)(6)+\\left[(8)(6)+(-5)(4)\\right]i=62+28i$.","explanation":"$(8)(4)-(-5)(6)+\\left[(8)(6)+(-5)(4)\\right]i=62+28i$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["0.5*(x - (1))**2 + (-2)","0.5*x + 1"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"point_labels":[{"x":1,"y":-2,"label":"$(1,-2)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Multiply $(2-2i)(-6-5i)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Addition and subtraction","choices":["$-22-2i$","$-22+2i$","$-2+2i$","$-22-22i$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$(2)(-6)-(-2)(-5)+\\left[(2)(-5)+(-2)(-6)\\right]i=-22+2i$.","explanation":"$(2)(-6)-(-2)(-5)+\\left[(2)(-5)+(-2)(-6)\\right]i=-22+2i$."},{"q":"Multiply $(-7-6i)(2+3i)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiplication","choices":["$4-9i$","$4-33i$","$4-32i$","$-32-33i$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$(-7)(2)-(-6)(3)+\\left[(-7)(3)+(-6)(2)\\right]i=4-33i$.","explanation":"$(-7)(2)-(-6)(3)+\\left[(-7)(3)+(-6)(2)\\right]i=4-33i$."},{"q":"Simplify $\\frac{6+8i}{2+8i}$ by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Conjugate and modulus","choices":["$-8/17+19/17i$","$19/17+8/17i$","$19/17-8/17i$","$36/17-8/17i$"],"correct":2,"answer":"Multiply by $(2-8i)/(2-8i)$: numerator $=(6)(2)-(8)(-8)+\\left[(8)(2)+(6)(-8)\\right]i=76-32i$; denominator $=(2)^2+(8)^2=68$. Quotient: $19/17-8/17i$.","explanation":"Multiply by $(2-8i)/(2-8i)$: numerator $=(6)(2)-(8)(-8)+\\left[(8)(2)+(6)(-8)\\right]i=76-32i$; denominator $=(2)^2+(8)^2=68$. Quotient: $19/17-8/17i$."},{"q":"Simplify $\\frac{16+4i}{4+2i}$ by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Division","choices":["$18/5-4/5i$","$-4/5+18/5i$","$18/5+4/5i$","$23/5-4/5i$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Multiply by $(4-2i)/(4-2i)$: numerator $=(16)(4)-(4)(-2)+\\left[(4)(4)+(16)(-2)\\right]i=72-16i$; denominator $=(4)^2+(2)^2=20$. Quotient: $18/5-4/5i$.","explanation":"Multiply by $(4-2i)/(4-2i)$: numerator $=(16)(4)-(4)(-2)+\\left[(4)(4)+(16)(-2)\\right]i=72-16i$; denominator $=(4)^2+(2)^2=20$. Quotient: $18/5-4/5i$."}]},{"heading":"Part VI — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","questions":[{"q":"For $z=9-40i$, find $|z|$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Quadratic equations with complex roots","choices":["$49$","$42$","$40$","$41$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$|z|=\\sqrt{(9)^2+(-40)^2}=\\sqrt{81+1600}=\\sqrt{1681}=41$.","explanation":"$|z|=\\sqrt{(9)^2+(-40)^2}=\\sqrt{81+1600}=\\sqrt{1681}=41$.","cross_lesson":"4.01 Advanced Functions & Equations"},{"q":"For $z=5+12i$, find $|z|$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","choices":["$17$","$12$","$14$","$13$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$|z|=\\sqrt{(5)^2+(12)^2}=\\sqrt{25+144}=\\sqrt{169}=13$.","explanation":"$|z|=\\sqrt{(5)^2+(12)^2}=\\sqrt{25+144}=\\sqrt{169}=13$.","cross_lesson":"5.01 Trigonometry & Polar Coordinates","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["0.5*(x - (1))**2 + (-2)","0.5*x + 1"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"point_labels":[{"x":1,"y":-2,"label":"$(1,-2)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"For $z=8-15i$, find $|z|$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","choices":["$17$","$18$","$16$","$23$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$|z|=\\sqrt{(8)^2+(-15)^2}=\\sqrt{64+225}=\\sqrt{289}=17$.","explanation":"$|z|=\\sqrt{(8)^2+(-15)^2}=\\sqrt{64+225}=\\sqrt{289}=17$.","cross_lesson":"6.01 Limits"},{"q":"Solve $x^2-14x+113=0$ using the quadratic formula.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","choices":["$7+8i$","$-7+8i,-7-8i$","$8+8i,8-8i$","$7+8i,7-8i$"],"correct":3,"answer":"Discriminant $=(-14)^2-4(113)=196-452=-256$, so $\\sqrt{-256}=16i$. $x=(14\\pm16i)/2=7+8i,7-8i$.","explanation":"Discriminant $=(-14)^2-4(113)=196-452=-256$, so $\\sqrt{-256}=16i$. $x=(14\\pm16i)/2=7+8i,7-8i$.","cross_lesson":"2.01 Matrices & Vectors"},{"q":"Solve $x^2+14x+85=0$ using the quadratic formula.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$-7+6i$","$-7+6i,-7-6i$","$-6+6i,-6-6i$","$7+6i,7-6i$"],"correct":1,"answer":"Discriminant $=(14)^2-4(85)=196-340=-144$, so $\\sqrt{-144}=12i$. $x=(-14\\pm12i)/2=-7+6i,-7-6i$.","explanation":"Discriminant $=(14)^2-4(85)=196-340=-144$, so $\\sqrt{-144}=12i$. $x=(-14\\pm12i)/2=-7+6i,-7-6i$.","cross_lesson":"3.01 Sequences & Series"},{"q":"Simplify $\\frac{6+8i}{2+8i}$ by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["$-8/17+19/17i$","$19/17+8/17i$","$19/17-8/17i$","$36/17-8/17i$"],"correct":2,"answer":"Multiply by $(2-8i)/(2-8i)$: numerator $=(6)(2)-(8)(-8)+\\left[(8)(2)+(6)(-8)\\right]i=76-32i$; denominator $=(2)^2+(8)^2=68$. Quotient: $19/17-8/17i$.","explanation":"Multiply by $(2-8i)/(2-8i)$: numerator $=(6)(2)-(8)(-8)+\\left[(8)(2)+(6)(-8)\\right]i=76-32i$; denominator $=(2)^2+(8)^2=68$. Quotient: $19/17-8/17i$.","cross_lesson":"4.01 Advanced Functions & Equations"},{"q":"What is the polar (trigonometric) form of the complex number $z = -2 + 2i\\sqrt{3}$?","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","choices":["$4(\\cos\\frac{2\\pi}{3} + i\\sin\\frac{2\\pi}{3})$","$4(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{3} + i\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{3})$","$2(\\cos\\frac{2\\pi}{3} + i\\sin\\frac{2\\pi}{3})$","$4(\\cos\\frac{5\\pi}{6} + i\\sin\\frac{5\\pi}{6})$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$r = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (2\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 12} = 4$. Angle $\\theta$ is in Quadrant II: $\\theta = \\pi - \\arctan(\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$.","explanation":"$r = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (2\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 12} = 4$. Angle $\\theta$ is in Quadrant II: $\\theta = \\pi - \\arctan(\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$.","cross_lesson":"5.01 Trigonometry & Polar Coordinates"},{"q":"What is the real part of the complex number $(2 - 2i)^4$?","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","choices":["$-64$","$64$","$0$","$-32$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$2 - 2i = \\sqrt{8} e^{-i\\pi/4}$. Raising to 4th power: $(\\sqrt{8})^4 e^{-i\\pi} = 64(-1) = -64$.","explanation":"$2 - 2i = \\sqrt{8} e^{-i\\pi/4}$. Raising to 4th power: $(\\sqrt{8})^4 e^{-i\\pi} = 64(-1) = -64$.","cross_lesson":"6.01 Limits"}]}]}